 # process-email
 
-One pass takes the single oldest message in your inbox, does whatever
-it asks, and answers it on the list. The answer is always a reply; any
-patches go out first as their own series, which the reply cites. Loop
-the skill to drain the inbox.
+One pass takes a single message in your inbox, the one its argument
+names or else the oldest, does whatever it asks, and answers it on the
+list. The answer is always a reply; any patches go out first as their
+own series, which the reply cites. Loop the skill to drain the inbox.
 
 The list is a maildir carried by jj on the `mail` bookmark.
 zib/patch-review-workflow.org describes the whole workflow; this skill
@@ -91,16 +93,25 @@ A bookmark with no pending mail, missing or merged in full, is
 pointed at the list's tip first. It has nothing to rebase, so the
 rebase alone would leave it behind on an old list.
 
-## 2. Pick the oldest inbox message
+## 2. Pick the message
 
-`just get-oldest-inbox-message` names it. Ask it rather than querying
-notmuch yourself, so every caller agrees on which message is next:
+The skill's argument, if given, names the message to answer as a
+notmuch id, `id:<message-id>`. Without one, take the oldest inbox
+message. `just get-oldest-inbox-message` names it. Ask it rather than
+querying notmuch yourself, so every caller agrees on which message is
+next:
 
-    id="$(just -f ~/lab/default.jj/Justfile get-oldest-inbox-message)"
+    id="$ARGUMENTS"
+    if [ -z "${id}" ]; then
+        id="$(just -f ~/lab/default.jj/Justfile get-oldest-inbox-message)"
+    fi
     thread="$(notmuch search --output=threads "${id}")"
     msgid="${id#id:}"      # the bare Message-ID, for headers
 
 If there is none, the inbox is empty: stop without writing anything.
+If the named message is not tagged `inbox`
+(`notmuch count "${id} and tag:inbox"` prints 0), it is already
+answered or archived: stop without writing anything.
 
 Every inbox message gets an answer, even if it is a short
 acknowledgement. Replying is the only way a message leaves the inbox,
